## Cursor Blink Interval Patterns in Popular Text Editors and Terminal Emulators

Based on my analysis of major projects, here are the common cursor blink interval values and implementation patterns:

### Default Blink Intervals

| Project | Default | Unit | Notes |
|---------|---------|------|-------|
| **Alacritty** | [750](https://github.com/alacritty/alacritty/blob/master/alacritty/src/config/cursor.rs#L33) | milliseconds | Configurable per cursor settings |
| **WezTerm** | 1000 | milliseconds | (Derived from easing function defaults; 200ms mentioned in animated image commit) |
| **Kitty** | -1 (system default) | seconds | Supports CSS easing functions for animation |
| **Zed (terminal)** | [500](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/terminal_view/src/terminal_view.rs#L68) | milliseconds | Editor uses boolean toggle instead of interval |
| **VSCode (editor)** | Uses style options | N/A | `blink`, `smooth`, `phase`, `expand`, `solid` |
| **VSCode (terminal)** | [false](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/main/src/vs/workbench/contrib/terminal/common/terminalConfiguration.ts#L288-L289) | boolean | No blinking by default in terminal |
| **Helix** | Via modifiers | N/A | `slow_blink`, `rapid_blink` theme attributes |

### Key Implementation Patterns

#### 1. **Alacritty** (Rust) - Time-based with Minimum Threshold
```rust
pub struct Cursor {
    blink_interval: u64,  // milliseconds
    blink_timeout: u8,    // seconds of inactivity before stopping
}

// Default: 750ms, minimum enforced: 10ms
const MIN_BLINK_INTERVAL: u64 = 10;

pub fn blink_timeout(self) -> Duration {
    if self.blink_timeout == 0 {
        Duration::ZERO
    } else {
        // Show/hide is one cycle, so multiply by 2
        cmp::max(
            Duration::from_millis(self.blink_interval * 2 * MIN_BLINK_CYCLES_BEFORE_PAUSE),
            Duration::from_secs(self.blink_timeout as u64),
        )
    }
}
```

**Pattern**: Supports configurable blinking with both interval and timeout (pause after N seconds of inactivity)

#### 2. **WezTerm** (Rust) - Easing Function-based Animation
```rust
#[dynamic(default = "default_cursor_blink_rate")]
pub cursor_blink_rate: u64,

#[dynamic(default = "linear_ease")]
pub cursor_blink_ease_in: EasingFunction,

// Supporting animated blinking rather than binary on/off
pub cursor_blink_ease_out: EasingFunction,
```

**Pattern**: Uses easing functions (CSS-style) for smooth animations. Supports functions like `Linear`, `ease-in-out`, etc.

#### 3. **Kitty** (C/Python) - System-aware with Animation Support
```python
opt('cursor_blink_interval', '-1',
    option_type='cursor_blink_interval',
    long_text='''
    The interval to blink the cursor (in seconds). Set to zero to disable blinking.
    Negative values mean use system default.
    ''')
```

**Pattern**: Respects system cursor blink settings; supports CSS easing functions; minimum limited by `repaint_delay`

#### 4. **Zed** (Rust) - Task-based Blinking
```rust
const CURSOR_BLINK_INTERVAL: Duration = Duration::from_millis(500);

pub struct BlinkManager {
    blink_interval: Duration,
    visible: bool,
    enabled: bool,
    blinking_paused: bool,
}

// Uses async tasks with Timer to manage blinking
cx.spawn(async move |this, cx| {
    Timer::after(interval).await;
    // Toggle visibility
})
```

**Pattern**: Uses async task scheduling for blink timing; supports pause on inactivity

#### 5. **VSCode** - Multiple Cursor Blink Styles
```typescript
// Editor uses animation styles
cursorBlinking?: 'blink' | 'smooth' | 'phase' | 'expand' | 'solid'

// Terminal uses boolean
[TerminalSettingId.CursorBlinking]: {
    description: "Controls whether the terminal cursor blinks",
    type: 'boolean',
    default: false
}
```

**Pattern**: Different approaches for editor vs terminal; rich style options for editor

#### 6. **Helix** - Theme-based Modifiers
```rust
pub enum Modifier {
    // ...
    SLOW_BLINK = 0b0000_0001_0000,
    RAPID_BLINK = 0b0000_0010_0000,
}

// Applied via theme configuration
"ui.cursor.primary" = {
    modifiers = ["reversed", "slow_blink"]
}
```

**Pattern**: Blink is a visual modifier applied through the theme system

### Common Design Considerations

1. **Minimum Interval Enforcement**: Most implementations enforce a minimum blink interval (10-50ms) to avoid excessive repainting and performance issues

2. **Inactivity-based Pause**: Alacritty and Zed both support pausing blinking after keyboard inactivity (typically 5-15 seconds), improving user experience during reading

3. **System Integration**: Kitty and macOS-based terminals can query system cursor blink preferences as defaults

4. **Animation Support**: Modern implementations (WezTerm, Kitty) use easing functions instead of simple on/off blinking for smoother visual appearance

5. **Configuration Flexibility**: Most allow disabling blinking entirely (setting to 0 or `false`)

6. **Performance**: Blinking is integrated with the repaint cycle to avoid unnecessary screen updates

### Recommended Values for Your Rust Text Editor

For a Rust implementation, a recommended approach would be:

```rust
pub struct CursorConfig {
    /// Blink interval in milliseconds
    pub blink_interval: u64,
    /// Whether blinking is enabled
    pub enabled: bool,
    /// Stop blinking after N seconds of inactivity (0 = never)
    pub stop_after: u64,
}

impl Default for CursorConfig {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            blink_interval: 750,    // ~500ms on/off cycle (similar to terminals)
            enabled: true,
            stop_after: 5,          // seconds
        }
    }
}

impl CursorConfig {
    pub fn min_interval(&self) -> u64 {
        std::cmp::max(self.blink_interval, 10)  // Enforce 10ms minimum
    }
}
```

